  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "W2SDFXlTT+UXgw5gBSRq0NhrF5M=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:32:29.401Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Digitaal Vlaanderen is the Flemish digital government agency: 640 staff, 326 public GitHub repositories, and publisher of the Vlaamse bouwstenen, Flanders' shared digital building blocks.\n\nThe research draws its conclusion from the repository count specifically. A published open-source policy costs nothing and is common across European public administrations, whereas 326 public repositories is the by-product of people working in the open routinely, which is much harder to manufacture. The number is used as evidence about operating culture, not as a measure of code quality or of anything else.\n\nThat matters for how the lead is approached. An agency already working in public can evaluate an openly licensed record layer on its merits without first settling a procurement argument about licence models, and as publisher of the bouwstenen it is positioned to make a component available to others rather than merely consume one. Like the other Benelux public bodies in the corpus it sits on a parallel track \u2014 institution, not supplier.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**1 \u00b7 parallel track**\n- Digitaal Vlaanderen (BE)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Flemish digital government agency: 640 staff, 326 public GitHub repositories, publishes the Vlaamse bouwstenen. \u2014 326 public repos indicates a genuine open-source operating culture, not just a policy statement.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:32:29.401242Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:32:29.401246Z"
    }
  },
